<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Merry Christmas, 1985</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Merry Christmas, 1985&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;South Atlantic Byte (Southeastern U.S. Coast)&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Wednesday, 25 December 1985, 05:00 UTC / 00:00 EDT&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;1 day Duration&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Al Sandrik / Beirutdude&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;On Christmas Eve of 1985 the ongoing Middle East crisis erupted into full scale global war with Warsaw Pact forces crossing the Inter-German Frontier and assaulting NATO Forces worldwide as of 10:00 UTC. Intel indicated a Soviet Victor I was southbound and moving in the direction of the Kings Bay Submarine Base and Naval Station Mayport possibly to interdict forces sortieing out of Mayport or Kings Bay.&amp;nbsp;VP-30 out of Naval Air Station Jacksonville was assigned to&amp;nbsp;detect and neutralize the threat posed by the Victor. USS Mahan DDG-42 is patrolling outside of Mayport in case the Victor breaks free.&amp;nbsp;Weather conditions are extremely poor with&amp;nbsp;a gale center west of Bermuda and strong high pressure centered over Ohio. The gradient flow between the two is causing near gale conditions over the Jacksonville and Charleston Operations Areas (OpArea) with seas of 12 to 18 feet. Heavy shipping/biologics are also in the area, further increasing the ambient noise in the water column, reducing the chances of detection. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = "o" ns = "urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Honestly this is a really simple scenario, in fact if I could give it a complexity of "1/2" I would.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Here's were I see it's value, Command just&amp;nbsp;doesn't handle air ASW missions well at all, it doesn't fly logical patterns and then it drops active sonobuoys at inappropriate times. My suggestion is to take personal control of the Orions and "mow the yard" with back and forth patterns dropping passive buoys only and alternating them with shallow and&amp;nbsp;deep strings with an overlap on both so you have a &lt;SPAN style='FONT-SIZE: 11pt; FONT-FAMILY: "Calibri",sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: "Times New Roman"; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA'&gt;continuous &lt;/SPAN&gt;detection area both shallow and deep. Then when you actually have a submarine contact drop a hexegonal/circular passive sonobuoy pattern around the sub until you are ready to attack. That is actually how we did it, even in the 80s. Nobody&amp;nbsp;drops active buoys until they are ready to attack, unless that is all they have, or they want the sub to know they are there!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;INI and Delta files are provide for rebuilds&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;A href="mailto:al.sandrik@gmail.com"&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;al.sandrik@gmail.com&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
